oring. This penalises the strain's breadth, Gini, and read count.&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a;padding:0 4px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700'&gt;Species&lt;/span&gt; — aggregates all strains sharing the same species. Uses a &lt;i&gt;union covered-bp&lt;/i&gt; approach: reads ambiguous &lt;i&gt;between strains of the same species&lt;/i&gt; are re-included because they confirm the species even without pinpointing the strain.&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='background:#e8f5e9;color:#2e7d32;padding:0 4px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700'&gt;Genus&lt;/span&gt; — aggregates all species in the genus using the same union logic at the genus level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style='color:#ffd580'&gt;Why Strain 72 / Species 75.8?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='font-size:0.85em;color:#ccc'&gt;Example: &lt;b&gt;Ⓓ Oribacterium sinus&lt;/b&gt; Strain 72 → Species 75.8. At strain level, reads overlapping conserved genomic regions shared with sibling strains are removed, lowering breadth and Gini. At species level those same reads re-count (they confirm the species), so species coverage and TASS are higher. This is expected behaviour — a strain can score below its parent species whenever intra-species read sharing is detected.&lt;/span&gt;"
